      sources: "Sources: Learn, Manage rules for dynamic membership groups (direct reports can't be combined). Book 03, Ch 10. Chapter 04, “Lint.”",
      say: `The best proof a linter is useful is that it finds something real. Run against Book 3's own table of rule patterns, it found three things. One pattern had no guest guard, so a guest with a matching employer name could have qualified; that's now fixed. Another let in a worker with an empty type, and the owner decided that shouldn't count, so it gained a clause. And the direct reports rule can't carry guards at all, because Learn says it can't be combined with anything, so the linter just notes it.`,
      detail: `Run against Book 03, Chapter 10's own rule pattern table, the linter found three things. The Legal employer pattern had no userType guard, so a guest whose companyName matched would have qualified (DR-04); it now has the guard, fixed in the same change as this chapter. It still reads companyName, a display-name attribute (DR-05), because the row exists to show that case. The Exclusion pattern admitted a worker whose type slot was empty (DR-10). The owner decided that an empty worker type must not qualify, so the pattern now ends -and (user.extensionAttribute11 -ne null), as this library's entry already did, and the linter finds nothing in it. The Direct reports pattern can't carry guards at all, because Learn says that rule can't be combined with any other, so the linter only notes it (DR-03, Info). Direct reports: can't be combined with other rules; adds the manager too. A test checks that the fixture still matches Book 03's table row for row.`,
