      say: `Who is this for? Developers build and maintain the library and its checks. Identity engineers own the group library, and every rule file names the owner who approves its changes. Reviewers approve each change, with the shadow diff in front of them. And each step asks for the least it needs. Creating groups needs group write rights with the Groups Administrator role. Testing a rule with Graph needs only read rights when you supply the rule. Pausing administrative units is the heavier one: it needs the Privileged Role Administrator.`,
      detail: `Audience: developers and identity engineers who own the group library, and the reviewers who approve rule changes. You should be comfortable in PowerShell 7. You don't need a tenant until Lab 2. The owner in each rule file is the person who approves changes. Creating groups needs Group.ReadWrite.All delegated (or Group.Create for an application) with Groups Administrator. Reading test results with Graph's evaluator needs only User.Read.All (or Device.Read.All) when you supply the rule, plus Group.Read.All with a group ID; the action is beta and delegated only, and is listed for the global service, US Government L4 and L5. Learn's samples request only the scopes for the phases you choose: Group.ReadWrite.All with Groups Administrator, AdministrativeUnit.ReadWrite.All with Privileged Role Administrator. Pause and resume: membershipRuleProcessingState On or Paused.`,
