      say: `Now the part that makes the case for all this. Run the pull request check and it passes cleanly: six rules, no findings. Then break a rule on purpose by deleting the clause that keeps out empty worker types, and run it again. Three things fire at once. The linter flags the empty-slot risk, the membership test for the worker with no type fails, and the shadow diff shows exactly who the change would let in. Put the clause back, run the whole suite, and seventy tests pass.`,
      detail: `Step 4, run the pull request check: pwsh ./powershell/Invoke-DrLibraryCheck.ps1 -BaseRef HEAD. Exit 0: six rules, no findings, no changed files. Step 5, break a rule on purpose. In rules/fin-led-not-contractors.json, delete " -and (user.extensionAttribute11 -ne null)" and run the check again. Exit 2. The linter notes DR-10, the test for u06 fails (DR-11), and the shadow diff prints "adds u06". Put the clause back. Step 6, run the tests: Invoke-Pester ./tests. Expected result: 70 tests passing, and each difference in the table above seen with your own eyes.`,
